<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Spanish Puerto Rico</h1><p class="st-meta">1508 CE – 1898 CE · capital San Juan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Spain's island colony and fortress in the eastern Caribbean for four centuries, guarding the route of the treasure fleets until 1898.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-ponce-de-leon">Juan Ponce de León</a> began the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> in 1508, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caparra">Caparra</a>; the settlement moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-juan-puerto-rico">San Juan</a> on its bay around 1521. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> were forced into gold mining, and their uprising of 1511 was crushed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>With little gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> became a military bastion, its fortresses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castillo-san-felipe-del-morro">El Morro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castillo-san-cristobal">San Cristóbal</a> paid for by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain</a>. It repelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Drake</a> in 1595, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in 1625 and the British in 1797, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-clifford-3rd-earl-of-cumberland">Earl of Cumberland</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-juan-puerto-rico">San Juan</a> briefly in 1598. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and tobacco grew in the 19th century, worked partly by enslaved Africans until abolition in 1873.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Grito de Lares revolt of 1868, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> granted autonomy in 1897, but the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> invaded in 1898 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish-American War</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1898">Treaty of Paris</a> transferred the island to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/puerto-rico-united-states">Puerto Rico (United States)</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/spanish-puerto-rico.jpg" alt="Spanish Puerto Rico" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: previous version User:Ignaciogavira ; current version HansenBCN, designs from SanchoPanzaXXI · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Spain_(1785%E2%80%931873,_1875%E2%80%931931).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1508 CE – 1898 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>San Juan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Juan Ponce de León</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Captaincy General of Puerto Rico, San Juan Bautista</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1703&amp;focus=spanish-puerto-rico" aria-label="Spanish Puerto Rico on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2889 1493 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Spanish Puerto Rico lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3209 1749L4910 989M3209 1749L3209 1749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M3209 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3209 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3209 1749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks San Juan. <a href="/atlas?year=1703&amp;focus=spanish-puerto-rico">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
